23	// Since Rust no longer uses jemalloc by default, ripgrep will, by default,
24	// use the system allocator. On Linux, this would normally be glibc's
25	// allocator, which is pretty good. In particular, ripgrep does not have a
26	// particularly allocation heavy workload, so there really isn't much
27	// difference (for ripgrep's purposes) between glibc's allocator and jemalloc.
28	//
29	// However, when ripgrep is built with musl, this means ripgrep will use musl's
30	// allocator, which appears to be substantially worse. (musl's goal is not to
31	// have the fastest version of everything. Its goal is to be small and amenable
32	// to static compilation.) Even though ripgrep isn't particularly allocation
33	// heavy, musl's allocator appears to slow down ripgrep quite a bit. Therefore,
34	// when building with musl, we use jemalloc.
35	//
36	// We don't unconditionally use jemalloc because it can be nice to use the
37	// system's default allocator by default. Moreover, jemalloc seems to increase
38	// compilation times by a bit.
39	//
40	// Moreover, we only do this on 64-bit systems since jemalloc doesn't support
41	// i686.
42	#[cfg(all(target_env = "musl", target_pointer_width = "64"))]
43	#[global_allocator]
44	static ALLOC: jemallocator::Jemalloc = jemallocator::Jemalloc;
